Every discipline can benefit from statistical expertise, but academia struggles to integrate statistics into disciplinary research training. In recent
years, academic institutions have developed data science support programs to provide statistical and data science consultations to their campus
communities. In ‘Creating Institutional Change in Data Science’, (The Moore Sloan Data Science Environments, 2017) scholars representing data
science centres at New York University, UC Berkeley and the University of Washington describe a ‘major cultural shift in universities towards
cross-disciplinary scholarship’, with data science cutting across needs surrounding the vast amounts, varieties, and applications of data specific to
each discipline (New York University, UC Berkeley, and the University of Washington, 2020). This paper, published around the time NC State
Libraries founded DVS, reflects the changing institutional priorities for many research universities across the United States. Often, statistical
consulting at academic institutions is housed in statistics departments, and other computer programming support is siloed in computer
science-related departments (Parker et al., 2021; Shao et al., 2021). Academic libraries offer a unique ‘home’ to statistical and data science con
sulting, due to their traditionally large role in undergraduate education support, and the campus-wide access of their services (Mani et al., 2021;
Shao et al., 2021).
The Data Science Consulting Service within the Data & Visualization Services (DVS) department at NC State University Libraries (The
Libraries) was launched to meet the growing needs surrounding data science education. Our aim is to highlight the distinctive aspects of our data
science technical service, including our collaboration with the Data Science Academy (DSA) at NC State and our graduate student staffing model.
The DSA, a university-wide interdisciplinary organisation, provides data science analytic support for NC State with its consulting program. Within
the broader context of data science-focused technical services at university libraries, we meet the needs of the university community through a
primarily peer-to-peer consulting service conducted by graduate students. Few universities follow the model we practice, so we will lay out a scan
of the current landscape of data science consulting at universities across the country to establish context. There are many advantages to this
model, and as a data-driven program, we will provide a summary of our assessment and needs evaluation process to elucidate how we stay ahead
of patron needs. We also describe our support model to illustrate the solutions we have discovered to best assist researchers in need of our
resources, and present potential future directions for the service. It is our hope that research libraries at other institutions will be able to
implement aspects of our model to improve their support frameworks in the future as well.
2 | LITERATURE REVIEW
There are a variety of staffing models used at data science centres and academic libraries to provide data science services to university patrons.
For many years, libraries have been the central place on campus to support researchers with needs that lie outside the scope of an academic
department, classroom, or laboratory. In ‘People Who Need Data People: A Case Study in Building Cross-Campus Data Support’, the authors
assert that as data science support centres become more common in research universities, investment in the human infrastructure for research
support must become more robust. Librarians are well situated to help address knowledge based needs, fill in knowledge gaps and build partner
ships with both academic departments and data science institutes on campus (Cook et al., 2022). This support requires both technical knowledge
as well as the professional skills needed to help coordinate assistance, provide consultations, and direct researchers to resources. While
researchers have expertise and peer support in their own fields, they may lack access to the broader support system available to them. Libraries
serve as physical spaces to conduct data science work as well as access points for staff to provide advising and their deep knowledge of available
resources.
According to Tenopir et al. (2017), the primary division in research support at academic libraries is informational consultation services and
technical hands-on services. Informational consultation services cover data management for funded studies and data management and sharing
plans (DMSP), while technical hands-on services cover data science techniques, data visualisation and GIS applications, among other technical
topics (Tenopir et al., 2017). For consultation services, NC State University Libraries provide research data management and DMSP help through
the newly formed Research Facilitation Service. For help with complex data analysis and statistical programming, our unit (Data & Visualization
Services) is directly responsible for providing data science-related instructional services and data science consulting (called technical services in
the literature). Joo and Schmidt found in their study that academic librarians may view skills around technical guidance (specifically data science
and data visualisation) as a lower priority for libraries to support (Joo & Schmidt, 2021); however, at NC State, we have found high demand for
this type of service.
The demand stems from a skills gap within the university curriculum. Students from fields with data science applications (outside of statistics
and computer science) are often not taught advanced technical skills within their academic programs that are currently required for their research.
Libraries have sought to fill this gap by offering instruction and/or consulting on the needed skills. For example, the investigation of data science skill
competence at NYU's academic medical centre, served by the NYU Health Sciences Library (NYUHSL), shows that students do not receive sufficient
hands-on technical training in data science through their academic programs (Surkis et al., 2017). The NYUHSL began data science instruction within
the medical centre to fill the gaps in students' knowledge, which shows that these library services are essential for research universities.
While data instruction at libraries is growing, it has been noted by Cox et al. (2017) that data librarians are often unable to keep up to date
with current trends in data science to support technical services around data science and data visualisation effectively (Cox et al., 2017). Some of
