3 | ACADEMIC DATA SERVICES IN FOCUS
To better understand models used in data science education and support at peer institutions, we scanned publicly available web content from
the libraries and/or data science centres of 46 four-year American academic institutions, including NC State. We compiled this selection of
institutions from Katz's 2018 study on Academic Data Science Centers in the United States, randomised subsets from SLOAN Grant recipients
from 2023, members of the Academic Data Science Alliance and additional recommendations from colleagues. Though our scan is not fully rep
resentative, we captured an understanding of data science services at a combination of public and private universities, varying in size, academic
offerings and cost. The sources tend to represent larger universities with a focus on data science and an interest in investing further in data
science education and resources for their campuses. Thus, the scan is skewed towards leadership institutions in statistics and data science or
who have already begun to expand their offerings. The vast majority of institutions assessed have the resources to maintain a dedicated data
science centre, statistics department that provides data services or qualifications, library-led data science department or a faculty data science
research collaboratory (n 1⁄4 43). Our sampling method is not inherently more representative of institutions with accessible online resources,
though it also omits information that is unavailable to the public. As a key factor of library and other campus-oriented educational services is
accessibility, however, we decided that online institutional resources would be suitable to capture general information about data science
resources. The following exploration of support models helps reveal how our services compare, as well as lays out the infrastructure of various
schools' programs.1
3.1 | Libraries and centres
Our findings reveal an array of support models and institutional efforts to promote data science education, most of which are well-oriented to
connect campus members to resources for curation and analysis. Among the institutions surveyed, all but one library (n 1⁄4 45) supported a research
data facilitation program through either a subject liaison or a dedicated data services department, though subject liaisons are not necessarily
always data services librarians or statisticians. All libraries represented (n 1⁄4 46) supported DMSP as well as provided data discovery assistance to
students and faculty. At institutions with more emphasis on technical support (n 1⁄4 28), small teams of librarians, not unlike our department at NC
State, provided a variety of data related services, tool support, course instruction and consultations with subject librarians.
We found that dedicated data science centres varied considerably in structure, scope and placement, often being housed in statistics depart
ments and computer science departments, IT centres or standalone data science initiatives. Within this framework, we define data science centres
as data science-focused campus initiatives to promote connections between statisticians or data scientists interested in sharing resources and
applications with interested or in-need campus members, though the size and range of supported audiences varies at each institution. Approxi
mately half of all institutions (n 1⁄4 24) had more than one data science initiative in addition to library services. Despite having a disciplinary focus
on data science, a very small number (n 1⁄4 4) of institutions provided more one-on-one assistance through a data science/statistics centre or
college than through their libraries. Libraries surveyed had strong DMSP resources in common, and many approached data services more holisti
cally as they made efforts to address a wide range of growing data science resource needs on their campuses. On the other hand, data science
centres demonstrated more distinct functions, particularly if they were research oriented and unattached to larger statistical or computational
education programs. In such cases, they often facilitated more peer-to-peer faculty and research support than open consulting.
There are a variety of factors that may influence what genre of service a university elects to support, and differences in inter-institutional
collaboration are difficult to tease out solely from public information in our scope. Due to this barrier, as well as our interest in capacity and
accessibility, we decided to hone in on the difference between ‘discrete’ and ‘comprehensive’ resources. To fall under a ‘comprehensive’ services
category, an entity needed to make their resources available to all members of the campus community and have at least one data specialist who is
positioned to provide help at several steps of the data lifecycle and analysis process. If these two characteristics were unmet, we labelled the
support model as ‘discrete’. Discrete models support a limited and sometimes more specialised scope when compared with an open consulting
model. Within this framework, we found nine major data support models from the universities in our scan (Table 1).
