4.1 | Service logistics
We have two service points in each of NC State's largest libraries: the Hill Library Data Experience Lab and the Hunt Library Dataspace. Online
and in-person consultation services are offered by the students who staff those labs during their shifts. We use a shared inbox to coordinate
requests from the two departments (DVS and DSA), as well as scheduling and communication software to ensure requests are resolved. Each of
these aspects contributes to the effectiveness of our support model.
4.1.1 | Service locations
The Data Science Consulting Service operates within two high performance data computing spaces situated in the Libraries (Figures 1 and 2). NC
State's Main Campus, where the Hill Library is located, is home to many departments within the College of Humanities and Social Sciences as well
as the College of Sciences. The Hunt Library is situated on Centennial Campus, close to the College of Engineering and similar departments. The
focus of requests from each computing space is influenced by the needs of patrons in programs closest to each library. The Hill Library's Data
Experience Lab (Figure 1) generally attracts more requests for R programming and statistics, while the Hunt Library's Dataspace (Figure 2) serves
researchers primarily from engineering and related fields. Our data centred software stack is curated to support data analysis, visualisation and
programming tasks for quantitative, image and time series data.
4.1.2 | Consultations
Users can book appointments with a specific Data Science Consultant or for a certain topic through the web-based scheduling platform, Calendly.
Consultants primarily provide short consultations (30 minutes) on data topics. Core technologies supported are R, MATLAB, Python, and GIS
