4.2.2 | Hiring process
We hire in conjunction with the DSA and begin by building a list of the most relevant skills and tools required to fill out the range of expertise for
the program. These skills are determined by needs not currently met by existing consultants and an assessment of the areas most requested by
our patrons each year. Details about skill analysis will be addressed further in the explanation of our assessment processes.
We advertise the positions across a wide variety of academic departments, focusing on those with expertise relevant to our service. This pro
cess generates a pool of shared candidates among DVS and the DSA. In our application form, candidates self-select the tools (e.g. Python, R, etc.)
and concepts (e.g. data cleaning and statistics) they feel comfortable consulting on. During the interview, we ask questions about their role in
certain projects where they used those tools/concepts, to help us evaluate their technical and professional skill sets. The students we select for
these positions help us handle a range of complex requests and coordinate between the two departments in our collaborative consulting model,
but we struggle to attain full coverage for our core tools (GIS in particular). We believe that a switch to fully funding consultants through Graduate
Research Assistantships (GRAs) will resolve this issue while increasing student employee retention.
4.2.3 | Training
Training begins with in-person group events that familiarise students with our service, introducing them to one another. Once students have been
trained for the daily requirements of the job, we continue to develop professional skills such as email communication strategies, data ethics and
consulting ethics and consultation meeting management techniques. In each semester, we introduce professional development during service
meetings through data science guest speakers and panels and facilitate students' presentations on their academic research or technical interests.
5 | SERVICE ASSESSMENT
We assess the reach and breadth of our service through analysis of scheduled consultations, email requests and counts of unscheduled interac
tions (web-based chat, drop-in questions) at our service points (Data Experience Lab and Dataspace) and follow-up surveys. Our data collection
approach also focuses on capturing the tools and concepts associated with each request for email and scheduled consultations. At the end of the
fiscal year, data are de-identified and cross-referenced with the NC State University database to match user information to their college and
status at the university (if applicable). While the consulting service started in 2018, the most recent fiscal years, 2022 and 2023, represent the
current joint-consulting model of our service, and we focus on those years in this assessment. Each fiscal year encompasses interactions made
from 1 June through 31 May of the following year. The fiscal year includes fall and spring semesters and both summer sessions.
5.1 | Counts by transaction type
The service conducts several types of transactions (information consultations with library staff) or requests: online consultations, in-person con
sultations, chat, drop-in consultations, email and unknown (those for which we do not have transaction data). We collect the location to compare
request volume for our library spaces and online.
We conducted a total of 1503 transactions in 2022 and 1976 in 2023. Volume increased dramatically between 2022 and 2023, and we
expect this trend to continue as the campus population expands and our services become better known.
Online consultations comprised the majority of scheduled consultations in both years (57% and 52% in fiscal years 2022 and 2023, respec
tively) (Figure 3). Our scheduling system, Calendly (https://calendly.com/), includes zoom-integrated online scheduling, making it straightforward
for patrons to take advantage of online consultation from anywhere, while the consultant is on shift in the lab. This includes affiliates at extension
offices, satellite campuses or distance education programs who cannot attend consultations within our on-campus spaces. We expect this to
remain a popular option, as it has been since the beginning of the COVID-19 pandemic.
The in-person and drop-in consultations take place in either the DXL or the Dataspace. Email requests, which are handled asynchronously,
usually within 2 business days, comprise approximately one third of total transactions. Note that a single email transaction may be multiple
exchanges between a patron and a staff member or consultant. We offer a synchronous chat service as well; however, these transactions make
up a small portion of the total, since our service is more effective through consultation and email rather than chat.
