5.4 | Space usage
We provide two data computing labs with a total of 20 Windows and five MacOS computers for NC State affiliates to use. Each workstation has
commonly used data science software, packages and libraries installed. Computer specifications vary somewhat, but they all have multicore Intel
Xeon processors, two terabyte storage drives, 64 or 128 Gigabytes of RAM and dual monitors. Additionally, all Windows computers include
NVIDIA graphics cards, commonly used in machine learning projects. Due to their high cost, these graphics cards are often difficult for students
to acquire. Since all the necessary components for their environment are ready, students prefer to come and prepare their models using these
computers rather than using their personal machines. Sometimes, we provide extended reservations to give our patrons enough time to train their
models. The extended reservation system has proven to be a vital resource for students engaged in intensive research or long-term projects. This
flexibility allows for uninterrupted work sessions, which is crucial for complex machine learning model training or detailed data analysis. We also
have spacious laptop stations to create a cohesive work environment for students who need extra monitors and a Wacom tablet with a
specialised drawing pen.
The availability of these specialised computer labs significantly enhances the learning and research capabilities of our patrons and students.
By offering access to high-end workstations, complete with essential software and powerful NVIDIA graphics cards, we bridge the technological
gap for many who otherwise couldn't afford these resources. Another use of our spaces is for group projects. Having multiple screens allows
multiple students to collaborate and work efficiently on shared tasks. The multimonitor setup is especially beneficial for data visualisation and
complex coding projects, where more screen real estate can enhance productivity. Our consultants mainly work within the labs, making drop-in
help possible with all the required resources readily available. In addition to the numbers reported about consistent drop-in consultations, our data
show the average time a patron spends in one session is around 2.8 h. Many also prefer to use our computers as short-term (up to 1 h) touchdown
stations. Fewer cases include much longer (multiple days) sessions. In two fiscal years, our labs had over 25,000 log-ins; over a third are from the
College of Engineering. They are followed by the Colleges of Sciences, Agriculture and Life Sciences, Natural Resources, and Poole College of
Management.
Overall, the specialised computer labs at NC State University Libraries provide essential technological resources and create a dynamic and
collaborative environment integral to our patrons' academic and professional growth. Around half of all libraries reviewed in our data services
environmental scan also maintained dedicated digital scholarship or data science labs, though with consistently less focus on high performance
computing as a library specific resource than NC State. The usage data and positive feedback we have received are a testament to the effective
ness and impact of our lab resources.
