6 | ADVANTAGES OF OUR MODEL
Librarian-staffed data science consulting programs face many challenges, including how to cover the data science learning needs of students in a
wide variety of disciplines. We believe our model, which addresses this issue, could be effectively replicated at other universities. The following
advantages are the result of our choice to run a student-led collaborative consulting service.
6.1 | Peer-to-peer consulting
Our primarily peer-to-peer consulting model, which is led by independent consultants who are guided by librarian staff, contributes to both
student success and development as well as providing NC State with multidisciplinary data science research assistance.
We staff our service points with a relatively large number of graduate student employees who are equipped to handle complex requests in
their disciplines and related fields. Since they are studying the most current models and techniques in their fields, graduate students are best
equipped to provide our patrons with up-to-date technical advice.
In addition, graduate student employees help to increase the accessibility of the service, since graduate student patrons are working with
consultants with similar levels of educational experience as them. The peer model also facilitates a lower pressure environment for patrons, since
they are more comfortable learning together and making mistakes with their peers than a faculty librarian. On the employee side, our open
request system exposes student consultants to questions and domains that they may have never considered before, leading to serendipitous
learning that helps build their skillset.
6.2 | Student success and development
The student employees work mostly independently, without an in-person lab supervisor and are guided by librarian mentors to develop the
professional skills required to conduct consultations effectively. Librarian supervisors have a background in data science or statistics and guide
the students through their development within the department.
Interpersonal skill sets, including teamwork, communication and creativity, are considered to be the most important skills possessed by data
support practitioners (Federer, 2022). These skills have been seen as less important in technical fields, but are essential for helping researchers
navigate the complex research environment and evaluating researchers' needs (Cook et al., 2022). Since interpersonal skills are required to provide
robust support to our patrons, we invest in developing them in our students. Our model encourages students to continue learning professional
skills, and broaden their technical skills while employed with us. This approach has paid off well for our students, many of whom have graduated
and started on data science-related career paths. In exit interviews, students have consistently mentioned that their time with the Data Science
Consulting Service was an advantage on their resume.
