student employees assisted by DVS and DSA staff.
7 | FUTURE IMPROVEMENTS
Our current support model has many advantages, and part of our process is to seek continuous improvements that match the evolution of our
service. We elicit feedback from our consultants each semester, asking them to share as anonymously as possible what they appreciated about
the service, constructive criticism, and suggestions for improvement. The consulting service team reviews the suggestions, responds and
implements the feedback that would benefit the service. Our assessment process as well as student feedback determines our plans for future
improvements. Two longer-term issues that we are working to resolve are improving the sustainability of our staffing model and developing more
robust campus partnerships.
7.1 | Sustainable staffing
One issue that has gotten gradually more challenging is student employee retention and competitive hiring. Over time, requests have become
more complex, often requiring expertise at the doctoral student level. Simultaneously, the department has struggled to offer competitive compen
sation packages to our students. The Data Science Consultant positions vary in compensation, since some are funded as assistantships which
provide tuition assistance and health insurance on top of an annual stipend, and others are paid hourly. We feel strongly that offering fully funded
GRAs to all consultants would create an attractive position for applicants. GRA positions have several advantages compared to the hourly posi
tions that currently make up the majority of our student positions: better retention, compensation, and the elevated expertise and professional
development opportunities that follow for the students. Having one position type will also improve equity among the students and allow us to
better integrate with the DSA.
Ideally, a team of GRA consultants would be able to handle our most challenging requests while building an open peer-to-peer culture among
consultants and patrons. DVS and DSA are moving to this model in the next fiscal year to resolve this issue and improve retention and student
employee equity. In moving to this new funding model, we are maintaining the mechanics of our support model as described above. In general,
we are changing only the funding structure for all student positions.
7.2 | Outreach and building robust campus partnerships
Our assessment indicates that our nascent service reaches across many disciplines. We are encouraged by the increasing numbers of requests to
our service; however, our assessment highlights some potential areas for growth and outreach. Different user-groups use different aspects of our
consulting services (i.e., email, consultations, space/computing resources). As our service grows, we may need to balance hiring students with skill
sets that fit to disciplines we do not currently reach while also maintaining support for those colleges and departments that are already using our
service but are expected to grow (e.g. College of Engineering). In terms of scheduled consultations, we assist primarily graduate students and have
observed fewer appointments from undergraduates and faculty or staff. Undergraduates may be getting a larger amount of support from their
individual departments, teaching assistants, instructors or academic advisors. Thus, they might not seek out additional help on content and
therefore use our spaces and/or hardware to complete their work. Graduate students, on the other hand, are faced with research projects that
