8 | CONCLUSION
We believe that the collaborative consulting model between the DVS and the DSA presents a compelling staffing and consulting model for other
research universities to implement. While our scan on data science services does not establish a complete context, trends in both library services
and campus needs reflect an increased focus on data services as well as a desire for increased technical data curation and analytical services.
Other institutions provide a wide range of services at their libraries that are often greatly augmented by an academic department or data science
centres' additional connection and consulting services. Programs that involve student assistants offer more comprehensive support in comparison
with peers; however, few university libraries from our comparison of data services have found a similar solution for offering data science
consulting. Both our student-led model and collaboration with an on-campus data science centre could prove effective for those universities
looking to expand their technical research services.
The graduate student-staffed, primarily peer-to-peer consulting service presents a solution to many of the pressing challenges faced by
libraries and other academic institutions to provide comprehensive data science support services to the university community. By relying on
graduate students, we are able to cover a wide range of disciplines, maintain both long-term and short-term consulting support and contribute to
student success. Our experience indicates that equal compensation for student consultants, providing health insurance, tuition assistance and a
competitive stipend will lead to a successful program. The advantages of this funding model, which ensure sustainability of the program, are
increased retention of consultants, strong technical expertise and a more robust culture within the service.
Our department's experience illustrates that libraries at NC State University's peer institutions with existing technical research services
will be able to achieve similar successes, given that they are able to invest in funding and training graduate student consultants and recruiting
librarians with advanced technical skills to mentor them.
Our assessment process assists us in staying ahead of future challenges and trends in research support at our university, and we evaluate
service needs through our service metrics as well as eliciting and implementing feedback from the student employees. These tools help us
establish and train an effective, always evolving and improving collaborative consulting team prepared to adapt and respond to challenges over
the long term. We hope that data science consulting programs across the country will consider the decisions we have made to provide an
effective data science support service as they adapt to meet increasing demand from university researchers and other library patrons.
